2015-07-09 13:10 GMT+03:00 Pierre Abbat <phma@bezitopo.org>:

> On Thursday, July 09, 2015 02:08:06 la gleki wrote:
> > Any other complaints in the current system of ASCII names?
> > Wuzzy suggested {canlu bu} for "space" which is now included into the
> > system.
> >
> > "/" is now {fe'i bu}.
> >
> > http://mw.lojban.org/papri/ASCII_table_keys_names
> >
> > I'd like these names to be made official.
>
> "," is ce'o bu. What happened to slaka bu?
>

I remember changing to {ce'o bu} so that this part of ASCII table got at
least some structure. Otherwise ()*+,-./ loses its reference to
mathematics. Of course, this poses a question should full stop be {denpa
bu}. Maybe {mu'o bu}. Definitely we can use letteralize {.i} (sentence
separator).
Also full stop/comma are used as decimal separator and for groups of digits
like in "1,002.56" although this is not culturally neutral and can cause
confusion. Is there an ISO-standard for that? other than that {ki'o bu} and
{pi bu} could be used making
()
+-*/
.,
sets somewhat organized.




>
> If "$" is rupnu bu, what is "=C2=A4"?
>

runpusudu bu then? But the symbol "$"  is used for different currencies.


> I used to call ":" {pi'e bu}, but if there is no pi'e bu, that's fine.
>

it's used  in different places, both for pi'e and zo'u. i dont know what to
prefer.
Besides it has other uses.
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colon_(punctuation)
Maybe just {kolon bu} although then it's not not what to name semicolon.

Something that is worth {pi'e} in some cases like in DNS lookups can be
expressed using full stop.


> Pierre
> --
> li ze te'a ci vu'u ci bi'e te'a mu du
> li ci su'i ze te'a mu bi'e vu'u ci
